# Citation File Format: https://citation-file-format.github.io/
# Run `cffconvert -i CITATION.cff -f bibtex` to get a BibTeX entry.
cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Riprap: cited flood-evidence briefings for New York City places, from public data"
type: software
message: >-
  If you use Riprap in research, reporting or applied civic work, please
  cite it.
authors:
  - given-names: Adam
    family-names: Rahman
    name-particle: Munawar
    email: msrahmanadam@gmail.com
    affiliation: "Independent"
repository-code: "https://github.com/msradam/riprap"
url: "https://msradam.github.io/riprap/"
abstract: >-
  Riprap gathers public flood evidence for a New York City address, a
  question about one, or a community district, and cites every sentence
  to its source. NYC is the production deployment, with 23 public-record
  city, state and federal sources; Chicago, Seattle and Albany are
  experimental deployments on the same code. The briefing is built
  directly from the data, and a question is answered by rules over its
  words; an optional LLM can choose the answer's lead and facts instead,
  and code checks that choice against the evidence. Three experimental
  models fine-tuned by the author (a surge forecast for the Battery and
  two satellite layers) answer some questions about the future; their
  output is always labelled experimental, with its limits and tested
  accuracy. Thirteen disclosure checks drawn from
  FEMA, IPCC AR6, TCFD, ASTM E1527-21, EPA/CDC CERC, the AP Stylebook
  and the SPJ Code report on every briefing. Riprap reports evidence; it
  does not give advice or make regulatory flood determinations.
keywords:
  - civic-tech
  - flood-risk
  - climate-exposure
  - citation-grounded
  - grounded-llm
  - open-data
  - socrata
  - fema-nfhl
  - noaa-coops
  - npcc4
license: Apache-2.0
version: 0.8.0
date-released: "2026-10-01"

# How Riprap depends on prior work. We cite the data sources Riprap
# composes in each per-pebble manifest's `provenance` block (see
# deployments/<city>/manifests/*.yaml); the items below are the
# model and methodology references.
references:
  - type: software
    title: "Granite 4.1 Language Models"
    authors:
      - name: "IBM Research"
    year: 2025
    notes: "An optional LLM, used when the gallery's question entries were made with a model. Apache-2.0. https://huggingface.co/ibm-granite"
  - type: software
    title: "Granite TimeSeries TTM r2 (Tiny Time Mixers)"
    authors:
      - name: "IBM Research"
    year: 2024
    notes: "Base of the experimental Battery surge forecast, msradam/Granite-TTM-r2-Battery-Surge. Apache-2.0. https://huggingface.co/ibm-granite/granite-timeseries-ttm-r2"
  - type: software
    title: "Prithvi-EO-2.0: A Versatile Multi-Temporal Foundation Model for Earth Observation Applications"
    authors:
      - name: "NASA IMPACT and IBM Research"
    year: 2024
    notes: "Base of the experimental surface-water layer, msradam/Prithvi-EO-2.0-NYC-Pluvial. Apache-2.0. arXiv:2412.02732"
  - type: software
    title: "TerraMind 1.0"
    authors:
      - name: "IBM Research and the European Space Agency"
    year: 2025
    notes: "Base of the experimental land-cover layer, msradam/TerraMind-NYC-Adapters. Apache-2.0. https://huggingface.co/ibm-esa-geospatial/TerraMind-1.0-base"
  - type: software
    title: "riprap-models: reproduction of three NYC fine-tuned foundation models"
    authors:
      - given-names: Adam
        family-names: Rahman
        name-particle: Munawar
    year: 2026
    url: "https://github.com/msradam/riprap-models"
  - type: article
    title: "New York City Panel on Climate Change 2024 Report (NPCC4)"
    authors:
      - family-names: "Rosenzweig"
        given-names: "Cynthia"
    year: 2024
    journal: "Annals of the New York Academy of Sciences"
    volume: 1539
    doi: "10.1111/nyas.15116"
